"""Distractor proposals and blind interaction probes."""

DECOY_SYSTEM = "You craft red-herring DECOY spans for a privacy benchmark, in ENGLISH ONLY. A decoy is a SINGLE cue-shaped span (never a pair) planted in a message that TEMPTS a privacy detector to flag it, yet reveals NOTHING about the author's true value of any of the 8 attributes (age, gender, nationality, education, occupation, income, relationship, health).\n\nTHE ONE HARD RULE: a decoy must never let a cold reader recover the author's ACTUAL value of any attribute -- alone or combined with any real cue. Leaning toward a value the author does NOT have is allowed and often useful; revealing the value the author DOES have is forbidden.\n\nForm: a single span, 1-5 tokens preferred (8 max) -- noun phrase, compound noun, object, document, appointment, routine, timestamp, or short prepositional phrase (not a clause, sentence, or question). Match the form, length, and register of the real cue spans so the set blends into the real cue pool.\n\nStrategy -- aim for diversity and temptation, not bland filler; vary across the set:\n- NEAR-MISS (the workhorse; MOST decoys): mimics a real cue's content/form but fits almost everyone -- maximally tempting, pins nothing, stays consistent with the persona.\n- ADMIN-ARTIFACT: a universal form / card / reference / portal / procedure everyone encounters.\n- MUNDANE TEXTURE: concrete sensory or logistical realism with no demographic signal.\n- WRONG-DIRECTION (AT MOST ONE per message): a detail a detector reads as evidence for a value the author does NOT have; it must stay plausible for this author and not contradict the persona or the other decoys. Never plant multiple conflicting life-stage signals.\n\nNAMED ENTITIES: a real country-specific program, agency, place, scheme, or brand reveals nationality (and sometimes occupation or income) -- do NOT use real region-specific names; use invented, generic, or globally-ambiguous ones that point nowhere.\n\nThe set should be mostly near-miss plus some mundane/admin texture, with at most one wrong-direction herring -- never a bland all-empty set, never a pile of contradictory facts. Return strict valid JSON only: no markdown fences, no prose, no comments, no trailing commas."
DECOY_TEMPLATE = 'Task: write {n} diverse DECOY candidates for a {ctx} message. Mostly near-miss, some mundane/admin texture, at most one wrong-direction; never a bland all-empty set.\n\nRecipient (who the message is addressed to): {recipient}\nWhat this message is about: {context_description}\nUse the recipient and scenario ONLY to make each decoy read as natural, on-topic domain texture or jargon that plausibly belongs in THIS message -- on-topic bait is what makes a decoy tempting. This does NOT relax the hard rule: a decoy must still reveal none of the author\'s true values below, alone or combined with any real cue.\n\nAuthor\'s REAL values -- a decoy must reveal NONE of these:\n{truths}\n\nReal cue spans already planned (HIDE / KEEP / NEUTRAL) -- match their style/format/length/register, but do not reuse, paraphrase, or overlap with them:\n{realcues}\n\nFor each decoy give:\n- "decoy_id" (e.g. "d1")\n- "span" (exactly ONE short span)\n- "kind" (wrong-direction | near-miss | admin-artifact | mundane | jargon | object)\n- "tempting_score" (0.0-1.0: how likely a detector is tempted to flag it)\n- "why_tempting" (why a detector would flag it)\n- "why_nonleaking_cold" (why it reveals no true attribute value)\n\nReturn JSON exactly in this shape:\n{{"decoys":[{{"decoy_id":"d1","span":"<span>","kind":"near-miss","tempting_score":0.0,"why_tempting":"","why_nonleaking_cold":""}}]}}'
DECOY_AUDIT_SYSTEM = "You are a cold interaction auditor for red-herring decoys in a synthetic privacy benchmark. You do NOT know the author's true profile values and must not assume them. You see only the context type, selected real cue spans, and candidate decoys.\n\nFor each decoy, CHECK EVERY ONE of the 8 attributes INDEPENDENTLY -- age, gender, nationality, education, occupation, income, relationship, health -- not just the obvious one. In particular: a NAMED program / agency / scheme / place / brand usually reveals NATIONALITY (the country it belongs to), and sometimes occupation or income tier; a named institution can reveal education or occupation. Report a leak for ANY attribute a cold reader could pin, including these named-entity nationality/occupation leaks.\n\nDecoys are single spans. Audit two cases:\n1. the decoy span ALONE;\n2. the decoy span combined with EACH single real cue span.\n\nReport a leak candidate only when a cold reader would recover a CONCRETE value clearly above the attribute's base rate (for a binary attribute ~0.5 is chance, not a leak); the reported confidence is that probability.\n\nReturn strict valid JSON only: no markdown fences, no prose, no comments, no trailing commas."
DECOY_AUDIT_TEMPLATE = 'Context: a {ctx} message.\n\nSelected REAL cue spans already planned for the message. Policy labels are omitted for this cold audit; treat them only as text spans:\n{realcues}\n\nCandidate DECOYS:\n{decoys}\n\nFor each decoy_id, list any concrete attribute values a cold reader could infer from the tested evidence combinations. If a combination is only broadly compatible with many values and does not recover a concrete value, do not count it as a leak.\n\nFor each leak candidate, include:\n- "case": "singleton" | "decoy_realcue"\n- "attribute": one of age|gender|nationality|education|occupation|income|relationship|health\n- "guess": the value inferred by a cold reader\n- "confidence": 0.0 to 1.0\n- "evidence": the exact span combination\n- "realcue_span": the interacting real cue span, or "" if none\n\nReturn JSON:\n{{"d1":{{"leak_candidates":[],"reason":""}}}}'
